This is byte-indexing into a string — the exact bug class forbidden by the safety rules ("Never use byte indexing on strings"). If `event.ts` contains a multi-byte UTF-8 character within the first 10 bytes, `&event.ts[..10]` panics with a boundary error. The rich parser is defensive (tolerates malformed lines), so a JSONL line with a non-ASCII `ts` field would crash the whole `/risk validate`/`report` formatting path instead of being tolerated.

The fix is small and surgical:
```rust
let ts_short = if event.ts.len() >= 10 {
    let mut end = 10;
    while end > 0 && !event.ts.is_char_boundary(end) {
        end -= 1;
    }
    &event.ts[..end]
} else {
    &event.ts
};
```

